A common way to enroll the TPM is to bind to one of its PCR banks. These banks
store encrypted system information of various kinds, most notably the Secure
Boot status in PCR7. The TPM will compare current values against the ones in the
PCR banks and only unlock a secret when these values match. However, values can
regularly change due to actions that may not be related to security issues at
all - especially in a dual boot environment.

For that reason, instead of binding directly to PCR banks, we will bind to a
"PCR policy". A policy refers to the expected state of multiple PCR banks at
specific phases of the boot process. It will create a digest - which is a hash
of all expected PCR values at one of these specific stages - sign it with a
private key, and package it with the UKI. Then, when an encrypted volume with an
enrolled TPM2 secret is encountered during the boot process, the current state
of the PCR banks will be matched against all available digests in the UKI. If
one matches and the digest can be verified with the public key also packaged
into the UKI, the volume will be unlocked.

To enroll the policy, we will first generate our keys for signing.
